# Introduction

## Purpose of the Assignment

I followed the video to practice running confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) in `lavaan`. I used the SPSS Anxiety Questionnaire (SAQ-8) to compare the one-factor, two-factor, and second-order models discussed in the seminar.

## CFA and EFA

Exploratory factor analysis (EFA) is useful when we do not yet know how questionnaire items group together. Confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) starts with a proposed structure and tests how well it agrees with the data. In the seminar, EFA is described as a way to explore a new scale, while CFA is used to check a measurement structure that has already been proposed. CFA focuses on the relationship between observed questions and unobserved factors; structural equation modeling (SEM) can also examine relationships among factors.

# Data and Software

## Required Packages

```{r}
library(haven)
library(lavaan)
```

## Importing the SAQ-8 Data

The SPSS file contains 2,571 responses and 23 variables. Following the seminar, I used `q01` through `q08`. These questions cover reactions to statistics, SPSS, computers, and mathematics. Item `q03` describes being excited by standard deviations, unlike several other questions that express worry or difficulty. I kept that wording difference in mind when reading the negative correlations.

```{r}
dat <- read_sav("SAQ.sav")
dat <- as.data.frame(dat)

str(dat)
head(dat)
```

I used `str()` to check the variables and `head()` to view the first six rows.

## Examining Correlations and Covariances

```{r}
round(cor(dat[, 1:8]), 2)
round(cov(dat[, 3:5]), 2)
```

`q06` and `q07` have the strongest positive correlation (r = 0.51). Both concern computers: one asks about limited experience, and the other says that computers hate the respondent. `q03` and `q04` are negatively correlated (r = -0.38), which fits their opposite wording. The diagonal entries of the correlation matrix are all 1; in the covariance matrix, those entries are item variances. For `q03` and `q04`, the covariance is about -0.39. The correlations suggest that several questions are related, although they may not all be measuring exactly the same thing.

# CFA Concepts

## Observed Indicators and Latent Factors

The answers to `q01` through `q08` are the observed indicators. SPSS Anxiety is a latent factor: it is not a column measured directly in the data, but something the model estimates from the responses.

## The Measurement Model

The seminar relates CFA to linear regression:

$$y_i = \tau_i + \lambda_i \eta + \epsilon_i$$

Here, $y_i$ is an observed item, $\tau_i$ is its intercept, $\lambda_i$ is the factor loading, $\eta$ is the latent factor, and $\epsilon_i$ is the residual. The loading describes the item's relationship with the factor. The residual is the part of the item that the factor does not account for.

## The Model-Implied Covariance Matrix

$$\Sigma(\theta) = \Lambda\Psi\Lambda' + \Theta_\epsilon$$

In this expression, $\Lambda$ contains factor loadings, $\Psi$ describes factor variances and covariances, and $\Theta_\epsilon$ describes residual variances and covariances. This matrix gives the covariances predicted by the model. The fit statistics help assess how closely those predictions match the observed covariances.

## Path Diagrams

In UCLA's path diagrams, boxes represent questionnaire items and circles represent factors. Single-headed arrows show factor loadings; double-headed arrows indicate variances or covariances. Lin cautions against adding correlated residuals just to improve fit without a reason for expecting those residuals to be related.

## Identification and Degrees of Freedom

For $p$ observed items, the number of unique variances and covariances is:

$$\frac{p(p+1)}{2}$$

A model's degrees of freedom are calculated as:

$$df = \text{known values} - \text{free parameters}$$

| Identification | Degrees of freedom | Meaning |
|----|---:|----|
| Under-identified | Negative (in simple parameter counting) | There is not enough independent information to estimate the proposed parameters. |
| Just-identified | 0 | The model uses all available information and cannot be meaningfully tested for global fit. |
| Over-identified | Positive | The model has restrictions that can be tested against the data. |

For three items, there are $3(4)/2=6$ known variances and covariances. Before restrictions, the three-item model has 10 possible parameters: three loadings, one factor variance, and six unique residual variances and covariances. Setting the three residual covariances to zero and fixing either one loading or the factor variance to 1 leaves six free parameters. Therefore, $df=6-6=0$. This explains why the three-item model cannot be evaluated using an overall fit test.

# One-Factor CFA

## Three-Item Model: Marker-Variable Method

The first example asks whether `q03`, `q04`, and `q05` measure a single factor. In `lavaan`, the symbol `=~` means that a latent factor is measured by the items on its right.

```{r}
m1a <- 'f =~ q03 + q04 + q05'
onefac3items_a <- cfa(m1a, data = dat)
summary(onefac3items_a, standardized = TRUE)
```

By default, `lavaan` fixes the loading of `q03` to 1 to set the factor's scale. Since the model has zero degrees of freedom, its perfect fit does not tell us whether these three items form a useful scale.

## Three-Item Model: Variance Standardization

The second approach instead fixes the factor variance to 1 and estimates all three loadings. `NA*q03` frees the first loading, and `f ~~ 1*f` fixes the factor variance.

```{r}
m1b <- '
  f =~ NA*q03 + q04 + q05
  f ~~ 1*f
'
onefac3items_b <- cfa(m1b, data = dat)
summary(onefac3items_b, standardized = TRUE)
```

The unstandardized loadings now use a different scale. UCLA reports 0.583 for `q03`, -0.665 for `q04`, and -0.551 for `q05`. The negative signs make sense because `q03` is worded in a more positive direction than the other two items.

## Standardized Loadings and Residual Variances

```{r}
inspect(onefac3items_a, "cov.ov")
```

In the UCLA results, the fully standardized loadings (`Std.all`) for `q03`, `q04`, and `q05` are approximately 0.543, -0.701, and -0.572, respectively. The negative values for `q04` and `q05` are relative to the way the factor is oriented by `q03`, which says that standard deviations are exciting. `q04` has the largest absolute standardized loading (0.701), so it is more closely connected to this factor than `q03` (0.543) or `q05` (0.572). UCLA reports unstandardized residual variances of 0.815, 0.458, and 0.626 for these three items. The residual variance is what remains in each item after accounting for the factor. The seminar uses `inspect(..., "cov.ov")` to display the model-implied covariance matrix of the observed items. Because the three-item model is just-identified, it reproduces the sample covariances.

## Eight-Item One-Factor Model

Now the question is whether all eight items can be explained by one general SPSS Anxiety factor.

```{r}
m3a <- 'f =~ q01 + q02 + q03 + q04 + q05 + q06 + q07 + q08'
onefac8items_a <- cfa(m3a, data = dat, std.lv = TRUE)
summary(onefac8items_a, fit.measures = TRUE, standardized = TRUE)
```

### Model Fit

| Statistic          | UCLA result |
|--------------------|------------:|
| Chi-square         |     554.191 |
| Degrees of freedom |          20 |
| CFI                |       0.871 |
| TLI                |       0.819 |
| RMSEA              |       0.102 |

The chi-square test is sensitive to sample size, especially with 2,571 observations, so I would not use that statistic alone. However, CFI, TLI, and RMSEA also point toward poor fit. The standardized loading for `q02` is -0.233, the weakest in absolute value. Unlike the other questions, `q02` asks about friends judging someone for struggling with SPSS, which may be why it does not relate as strongly to the general factor. `q04` has the largest absolute loading (0.667). The model also leaves substantial residual variation in individual items. The poor fit and the weak loading for `q02` make the single-factor explanation difficult to support.

# Two-Factor CFA

In the next examples, UCLA keeps five items for an SPSS Anxiety factor (`q01`, `q03`, `q04`, `q05`, `q08`) and uses `q06` and `q07` for an Attribution Bias factor. Item `q02` is excluded from these models. The second factor has only two indicators, so UCLA adds a restriction to identify the uncorrelated model.

## Uncorrelated Two-Factor Model

This model forces the covariance between the two factors to zero. It also gives the two loadings on the two-indicator factor the same label (`a`), constraining them to be equal.

```{r}
m4a <- '
  f1 =~ q01 + q03 + q04 + q05 + q08
  f2 =~ a*q06 + a*q07
  f1 ~~ 0*f2
'
twofac7items_a <- cfa(m4a, data = dat, std.lv = TRUE)
summary(twofac7items_a, fit.measures = TRUE, standardized = TRUE)
```

UCLA reports chi-square 841.205 with 15 df, CFI 0.786, TLI 0.700, and RMSEA 0.146. The unstandardized `f2` loadings are equal because the model constrains them; the standardized loadings are 0.710 for `q06` and 0.723 for `q07`. For `f1`, `q04` is the strongest indicator here (0.687 standardized) and `q08` is the weakest (0.494). The fit statistics are still poor even though the items are divided into two factors. Dividing the items into two factors did not help much when their correlation was fixed at zero.

## Correlated Two-Factor Model

The next model allows the two factors to be correlated. This also helps identify the factor with two indicators, so the equality constraint on `q06` and `q07` is no longer necessary.

```{r}
m4b <- '
  f1 =~ q01 + q03 + q04 + q05 + q08
  f2 =~ q06 + q07
'
twofac7items_b <- cfa(m4b, data = dat, std.lv = TRUE)
summary(twofac7items_b, fit.measures = TRUE, standardized = TRUE)
```

This model allows `f1` and `f2` to correlate instead of fixing their covariance to zero. UCLA reports a factor correlation of 0.676. A correlation of 0.676 suggests that the two factors are related rather than independent. Among the `f2` items, `q07` has a stronger standardized loading (0.861) than `q06` (0.596). Their standardized residual variances are 0.258 and 0.644, respectively: more of `q06` remains unexplained by the factor. In this model, `q07` represents `f2` more strongly than `q06`.

## Comparing the Models

| Model                     | Items | Chi-square |  df |   CFI |   TLI | RMSEA |
|---------------------------|------:|-----------:|----:|------:|------:|------:|
| One factor                |     8 |    554.191 |  20 | 0.871 | 0.819 | 0.102 |
| Two factors, uncorrelated |     7 |    841.205 |  15 | 0.786 | 0.700 | 0.146 |
| Two factors, correlated   |     7 |     66.768 |  13 | 0.986 | 0.977 | 0.040 |

*Values from the UCLA seminar. The one-factor model uses eight items, while the two-factor models use seven.*

The correlated two-factor model fits these seven items much better than the uncorrelated model: CFI increases from 0.786 to 0.986, and RMSEA decreases from 0.146 to 0.040. I would not treat the eight-item and seven-item models as a direct comparison, since `q02` is excluded from the latter.

# Second-Order CFA

## Model Specification

A second-order factor is a broader latent construct that explains the association between first-order factors. UCLA calls the broader construct overall Anxiety. In this example, the two first-order factors are SPSS Anxiety and Attribution Bias.

```{r}
m5a <- '
  f1 =~ q01 + q03 + q04 + q05 + q08
  f2 =~ q06 + q07
  f3 =~ 1*f1 + 1*f2
  f3 ~~ f3
'
secondorder <- cfa(m5a, data = dat)
summary(secondorder, fit.measures = TRUE, standardized = TRUE)
```

UCLA fixes both higher-order loadings to 1 in this example, which helps identify a model with only two first-order factors.

## Interpretation

UCLA notes an estimation warning for this second-order model, so its parameter estimates need to be read carefully.

UCLA reports the same fit statistics as the correlated two-factor model: chi-square = 66.768, df = 13, CFI = 0.986, TLI = 0.977, and RMSEA = 0.040. Since the fit statistics are identical, this result does not show that the second-order model is better than the correlated two-factor model. With only two first-order factors, I would keep the simpler correlated-factor interpretation.

# Practice Questions and Answers

## 1. What is the difference between EFA and CFA?

EFA explores how items might group together without requiring a fully specified factor structure beforehand. CFA tests whether an already proposed structure fits the observed relationships among items.

## 2. What are the observed indicators and latent factors in SAQ-8?

The observed indicators are the eight questionnaire items, `q01` through `q08`. SPSS Anxiety is the latent construct in the one-factor example. Later, UCLA considers SPSS Anxiety and Attribution Bias as two first-order factors, with overall Anxiety as a possible second-order factor.

## 3. What do factor loadings represent?

A loading shows the relationship between an observed item and its latent factor. Its sign indicates direction, and its standardized magnitude indicates how strongly the item reflects the factor. A low absolute loading may suggest the item does not measure the proposed construct very well.

## 4. What do residual variances represent?

A residual variance represents the portion of an item's variability that the factor does not explain. An item can have a useful loading while still containing measurement error or other item-specific variation.

## 5. How many unique variances and covariances are available with three indicators?

There are $3(3+1)/2=6$: three item variances and three unique covariances. The other three off-diagonal cells repeat the same covariance values because the matrix is symmetric.

## 6. What is the difference between a fixed and a free parameter?

A fixed parameter has a value set in advance, such as a loading fixed to 1. A free parameter is estimated from the data. Fixing some parameters can help establish the measurement scale and identify a model.

## 7. Why is a model with negative degrees of freedom under-identified?

In basic parameter counting, negative degrees of freedom mean the model has more free parameters than independent observed covariance values. There is not enough information to uniquely determine all the estimates. Also, having zero or positive degrees of freedom is necessary but not always sufficient for full identification.

## 8. Why does fixing one loading and setting residual covariances to zero help identify a model?

Fixing one loading gives the latent factor a scale. Setting three residual covariances to zero removes three parameters. For the three-item example, this reduces the 10 possible unique parameters to six free parameters, matching the six known values and giving zero degrees of freedom.

## 9. What is the difference between just-identified and over-identified models?

A just-identified model has zero degrees of freedom and can reproduce the observed covariance structure without remaining testable restrictions. An over-identified model has positive degrees of freedom, which means we can evaluate how well its restrictions match the data.

## 10. How should chi-square, CFI, TLI, and RMSEA be interpreted?

Chi-square tests exact fit; a small p-value suggests the model's implied covariance matrix differs from the observed data, although this test is sensitive to sample size. CFI and TLI compare fit with a baseline model, and higher values generally mean better fit. RMSEA estimates approximate lack of fit per degree of freedom, so smaller values are better. UCLA discusses rules of thumb such as CFI around 0.90–0.95 or higher and RMSEA around 0.05–0.06 or lower, but these are guidelines rather than strict universal rules. I would consider them together with the loadings and the meaning of the items.

## 11. What is the difference between correlated and uncorrelated factors?

Uncorrelated factors are constrained to have zero covariance. Correlated factors are allowed to share variance. In UCLA's example, letting SPSS Anxiety and Attribution Bias correlate leads to substantially better fit.

## 12. What does a second-order factor represent?

A second-order factor is an underlying construct that helps explain why the first-order factors are related. In the seminar, overall Anxiety is proposed as a broader construct above SPSS Anxiety and Attribution Bias.

## 13. Which model appears most theoretically and statistically appropriate?

The correlated two-factor model is the most convincing of the demonstrated options based on the reported fit indices and the idea that the two types of anxiety-related responses can be related. Its CFI is 0.986 and RMSEA is 0.040. I would be more cautious about the second-order model because it has only two first-order factors and its fit is identical in this example. I also would not treat the one-factor and two-factor results as a fully controlled model comparison because the latter excludes item `q02`.

## 14. What additional information would help before making a final decision?

I would look more closely at `q02` before dropping it, because the question focuses on how friends might judge someone rather than how comfortable that person feels with SPSS. I would also check whether the two-factor structure holds with a different group of respondents, and whether the seven retained questions cover the parts of anxiety that the scale is supposed to measure. Finally, I would want to know whether treating the five-point responses as continuous is reasonable for these items.

# Summary of Important Lessons

The three-item model has zero degrees of freedom, so its perfect fit is not a reason to accept it. The eight-item model gives a more useful fit test, and its results raise questions about `q02`, which has a standardized loading of -0.233. For the seven-item models, allowing the factors to correlate improves RMSEA from 0.146 to 0.040. The comparison also shows why it matters to read the item wording and model restrictions, not just the fit statistics.

